The goal is the length of the longest contiguous stretch in which each element is at least the one before it, with 0 for an empty list and at least 1 otherwise.

After returning 0 for an empty list, the submission scans once with a current-run counter and a best-so-far value:

        for i in range(1, len(lst)):
            if lst[i] >= lst[i - 1]:
                current += 1
                if current > longest:
                    longest = current
            else:
                current = 1

Both counters start at 1, so any single element counts as a run. The comparison uses greater-than-or-equal, which is what makes equal neighbours extend a run rather than break it, as non-decreasing requires. A drop resets the current run to 1, since the element that caused the drop begins a new run. The best value is updated inside the loop whenever the current run exceeds it, so a longest run that ends at the final element is still recorded.

I traced [4, 2, 2, 5, 1, 3, 3, 3, 9], which none of the asserts use. The runs are [4], then [2, 2, 5] of length 3, then [1, 3, 3, 3, 9] of length 5. The counter reaches 3 at the 5, resets at the 1, then climbs to 5 at the final 9, updating longest to 5 on the last step. The function returns 5, which is correct. The code has no special handling for the example lists; it depends only on adjacent comparisons, so it generalises to any list of comparable values.

